sts/test_report_kit_references.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_report_read_doc.py", "pyproject.toml", "tests/enforcement/test_browser_marker.py", "tests/enforcement/test_ci_unit_shards.py", "tests/enforcement/test_testament_image_playwright.py"], "files_oracle": [], "findings": [], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 0, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The shard route and every release run now execute the 73 browser tests under THREETEARS_REQUIRE_CHROMIUM=1 on ubuntu-latest, with no evidence they pass there; fonts are the known failure mode", "recommendation": "instance. The evidence (73/73 three times) is the pool only. On the pool, 15 of 73 failed until fontconfig resolved sans-serif/system-ui/monospace to DejaVu. ubuntu-latest has its own preinstalled fonts plus the set playwright --with-deps adds, and fontconfig's sans-serif preference puts Noto Sans before DejaVu, so installing fonts-dejavu-core does not show that the runner resolves to DejaVu. release.yml always takes the shards (.github/workflows/release.yml:144-169), so a font mismatch would turn a tag's release run red. Fix: run the shard route once on this tree (route forced to the shards) and record the result. Or add an `fc-match sans-serif` / `fc-match monospace` assertion after the install step, so a wrong face fails at install, by name, rather than as 15 layout failures. Boundary rating: WARNING.", "files": [".github/workflows/ci.yml", ".github/workflows/release.yml"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Under the require flag, a launch probe cached per process turns one transient launch failure into a failure of every later browser test in that worker", "recommendation": "instance. packages/evals/tests/report_kit_support.py:83 @functools.cache caches the failure reason as well as success. On a busy pool runner (the brief reports SIGKILLs there), one failed launch now fails every remaining browser test in that process with the same reason, where before each test probed afresh. Cache success only (memoise `None`, re-probe after a failure), or retry the probe once before pytest.fail. Boundary rating: WARNING.", "files": ["packages/evals/tests/report_kit_support.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Nothing pins that chromium_unavailable() fails rather than skips when THREETEARS_REQUIRE_CHROMIUM=1", "recommendation": "instance. tests/enforcement/test_testament_image_playwright.py and test_ci_unit_shards.py pin that the manifest and the evals shards SET the variable. No test shows the helper honours it. The pool runs all pass, so they never take the pytest.fail branch. If that branch regressed to a skip, the skip-reads-as-a-pass failure this change exists to prevent would return silently. Fix: a pytest-in-subprocess test (pytester, or `sys.executable -m pytest` on a planted test) that runs with PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH pointing at an empty tmp dir and the flag set, and asserts the planted test FAILED and did not skip. That goes through the front door, with no private name. Boundary rating: WARNING.", "files": ["packages/evals/tests/report_kit_support.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "check.py's default path now waits on document.fonts with no time bound, and the --fonts abort path has no test", "recommendation": "instance. docs/eval-report-kit/scripts/check.py:35-39 and :315. For real users (no --fonts), SETTLED replaces a 1.2 s sleep that could never hang. Font loads started before `load` are already covered by goto's 30 s timeout. A face first requested by a post-load redraw that then stalls on the network would leave pg.evaluate waiting indefinitely, because Playwright's evaluate has no timeout. Bound it, for example with asyncio.wait_for around the evaluate, failing with a named reason. Separately, every test run passes --fonts, and none asserts that a non-font http(s) request under --fonts is aborted and reported as an error, which is the half of the flag's documented contract that makes it 'say what it would have fetched'. The semantics otherwise hold. drawCharts runs at DOMContentLoaded, before goto returns, so no check runs before a chart exists. The kit's own fonts.ready.then(redraw) was registered first, so it runs before SETTLED resumes. The two frames cover the ResizeObserver redraw after a scrollbar appears. The only transitions are the tooltip's (shown only on hover) and the scroll-timeline fade, which is not time-based. Boundary rating: NOTE.", "files": ["docs/eval-report-kit/scripts/check.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "The image pins playwright and the Chromium build by version, not by hash, and uvx resolves playwright's own dependencies unpinned", "recommendation": "instance. docker/testament.Dockerfile:33. The stanza above it pins uv and Python by digest. This one pins `playwright==1.60.0` only: greenlet and pyee float at each rebuild, the Chromium download has no integrity check, and the apt packages float, as the existing stanza's do. The browser build is fixed by browsers.json, so test behaviour is pinned. What stays open is whether a rebuild succeeds identically on both arches. Optional: install with a constraints file, or `uv tool run --with` exact pins. Boundary rating: NOTE.", "files": ["docker/testament.Dockerfile"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The CI step that reads the playwright version cannot fail, so an empty version yields cache key 'playwright-<os>-<arch>-'", "recommendation": "instance. .github/workflows/ci.yml:183-184 and release.yml:146-148. `echo \"version=$(uv run python -c ...)\"` succeeds even when the substitution fails, so the cache key loses its version and later versions restore a stale browser directory. Harmless to correctness, since `playwright install` fetches its own build, but it makes the cache meaningless. Assign first (`v=$(...)`), then echo, under bash -e. Boundary rating: NOTE.", "files": [".github/workflows/